Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://minutes-1.nwleics.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-12-10","committee":"Corporate Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://minutes-1.nwleics.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-08-20","committee":"Corporate Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Cancelled","webcast":false,"url":"https://minutes-1.nwleics.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-08-25","committee":"Cabinet","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://minutes-1.nwleics.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-10","committee":"Community Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://minutes-1.nwleics.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Council","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://minutes-1.nwleics.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://minutes-1.nwleics.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"North West Leicestershire flags housing account sustainability risk as reserves fall","lead":"North West Leicestershire's housing account faces sustainability risks as unrestricted reserves\u2014money the council can actually spend\u2014fall to critical levels, the council warned this week. Reserves stand at \u00a31.688m, only 68% above the statutory minimum of \u00a31.000m. Consecutive years of financial pressure have driven this decline, with operating expenditure, the cost of day-to-day running, exceeding forecast by \u00a30.293m in the first quarter of 2026/27 alone. Maintenance costs are running \u00a31.022m over budget due to increased repairs activity and \u00a30.204m in compensation for historic repair failures. The council has cut planned debt repayment by 27%, from \u00a31.319m to \u00a31.000m, to manage the pressure, while allocating \u00a30.735m to building reserves.","iso":"2026-09-22","paper_title":"HRA - Reserves Forecast","paper_url":"https://nwleics.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s52453/52453.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Housing account faces new year operating pressure despite debt repayment plans","lead":"North West Leicestershire's housing revenue account, which manages the council's own rented homes, is forecast to overspend on day-to-day running costs by \u00a3293,000 in its first quarter, the second consecutive year of such pressure. The council had budgeted to repay \u00a31.319m of borrowing this year but has cut that to \u00a31m, citing financial pressure on the account. The overspend is driven by higher-than-expected repair and maintenance costs\u2014\u00a31.022m above budget\u2014particularly on in-house repairs, heating systems, asbestos work and compensation for historic repair failures. The council is partly offsetting the pressure through an interest income windfall of \u00a3295,000 from favourable rates. Despite the setback, the council plans to allocate its net operating surplus of \u00a31.735m between the \u00a31m debt repayment and \u00a3735,000 towards rebuilding general reserves, which stood at only \u00a31.688m at the end of March.","iso":"2026-09-22","paper_title":"HRA - Reserves Forecast","paper_url":"https://nwleics.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s52453/52453.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"General Fund Finance Update - 2026/27 Quarter 1","paper_url":"https://nwleics.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s52443/52443.pdf","headline":"LGR Section 24 direction (expected January 2027) will require shadow authority approval for major contracts.","detail":"Under Local Government Reorganisation transition arrangements, from January 2027 when the Section 24 order is issued, capital contracts exceeding \u00a31.000m and new revenue contracts above \u00a30.100m will require approval from both the council and the new shadow authority. Officers warn this will impact project approval and procurement processes during the transition period.","category":"governance","matters":"Dual-approval requirements will slow procurement and project approval from January 2027 onwards. This creates risk to capital programme delivery and service change initiatives during a period of significant organisational restructuring, when speed and clarity are needed.","figures":"Capital contract threshold requiring dual approval: \u00a31.000m. Revenue contract threshold: \u00a30.100m. Section 24 order expected: January 2027.","quote":"Under LGR transition arrangements, once the Section 24 order is issued in the New Year (2027), capital contracts over \u00a31.000m and new revenue contracts above \u00a30.100m in value will have to be approved by both the Council and the new shadow authority.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"General Fund Finance Update - 2026/27 Quarter 1","paper_url":"https://nwleics.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s52443/52443.pdf","headline":"Additional pay award cost of \u00a30.050m can be absorbed within existing contingency.","detail":"The formal local government pay award for 2026/27 has been agreed at 3.3%, compared to the council's budget assumption of 3%, creating an additional cost of approximately \u00a30.050m. This can be met from the \u00a30.500m contingency budget (which had \u00a30.270m remaining after Q1 virements), enabling the council to meet employee obligations without impacting planned service delivery or requiring compensating reductions elsewhere.","category":"other","matters":"While the pay award creates a cost pressure, the council has sufficient contingency reserve to absorb it without service impact. This demonstrates retained budget flexibility. However, contingency funds are finite and are being drawn upon for other pressures.","figures":"Pay award cost difference: 3.3% awarded vs 3% budgeted = approximately \u00a30.050m. Original contingency budget: \u00a30.500m. Q1 balance after virements: \u00a30.270m.","quote":"The difference between the budgeted pay award and the current offer is estimated to result in an additional cost of approximately \u00a30.050m.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"General Fund Finance Update - 2026/27 Quarter 1","paper_url":"https://nwleics.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s52443/52443.pdf","headline":"Investment income forecast to exceed budget by \u00a30.818m (147% better than budgeted).","detail":"Investment interest income is forecast at \u00a31.159m against a budget of \u00a30.555m, a favourable variance of \u00a30.818m. The surplus is driven by the council holding higher than anticipated cash balances, which have been invested in well-paying Money Market Funds. General Fund investment income alone contributes \u00a30.637m vs a budget of \u00a30.210m. The forecast surplus is expected to be transferred to reserves.","category":"income","matters":"Strong investment income improves the in-year financial position and helps offset pressures from other areas. However, this surplus reflects higher cash holdings (potentially from S106 and other contributions held on behalf of third parties), not improved operational performance, and should not be assumed to recur.","figures":"Total forecast investment income: \u00a31.159m. Budgeted: \u00a30.555m. Variance: \u00a30.818m. General Fund: \u00a30.637m actual vs \u00a30.210m budgeted. HRA: \u00a30.522m vs \u00a30.345m.","quote":"Investment interest income is preforming better than budget. Investment income is forecast at \u00a31.159m compared to the budget of \u00a30.555m resulting in a surplus of \u00a30.818m.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"General Fund Finance Update - 2026/27 Quarter 1","paper_url":"https://nwleics.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s52443/52443.pdf","headline":"Whitwick Special Expenses remains in forecast deficit despite 50% precept increase.","detail":"Whitwick Special Expenses opened 2026/27 with a \u00a320k deficit balance and is forecast to worsen to \u00a321k deficit by year-end, despite the precept being increased by 50% in 2026/27 to help reduce it. The deterioration is driven by reduced cemetery income. Officers note there is limited expenditure that can be deferred, and a review is underway to identify improvement options.","category":"governance","matters":"A persistent and worsening deficit in a special expense area, despite aggressive precept increases, suggests underlying structural problems in service costs or delivery that cannot be resolved through pricing. This signals potential service viability issues.","figures":"Opening balance (1 April 2026): -\u00a320k. Forecast balance (31 March 2027): -\u00a321k. 2026/27 budget assumed \u00a33.3k contribution to balances; latest forecast is \u00a31k deficit. Precept increased 50% in 2026/27.","quote":"Whitwick Special Expenses is forecast to remain in deficit at year-end. The 2026/27 budget included a \u00a33.3k contribution back into balances; however, reduced cemetery income means the latest forecast is a \u00a31k deficit.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"General Fund Finance Update - 2026/27 Quarter 1","paper_url":"https://nwleics.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s52443/52443.pdf","headline":"Nearly half the capital programme budget (\u00a316.4m of \u00a334.0m) has been reprofiled into 2027/28.","detail":"Of the \u00a333.994m revised capital budget for 2026/27, only \u00a317.583m is forecast to be spent in-year, with \u00a316.412m reprofiled to 2027/28 (48.3% of the budget). Reasons include delays in procurement, scheme design phases not yet complete (Marlborough centre renovation, Moira furnace phase 2), and delays in securing approvals. A period 4 review further increased the reprofiled forecast to \u00a317.387m.","category":"capital","matters":"Significant capital reprofiling indicates delays in project delivery and procurement, which signals challenges in project management and delivery capability. For a lender, this affects confidence in the council's capacity to complete capital schemes on planned timescales and realise the intended infrastructure benefits.","figures":"Revised capital budget: \u00a333.994m. Q1 forecast outturn: \u00a317.583m. Reprofiled to 2027/28: \u00a316.412m (48.3%). Updated period 4 forecast: \u00a316.625m outturn, \u00a317.387m reprofiled.","quote":"\u00a316.412m of the budget is currently expected to be re-profiled to 2027/28.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-22"},"name":"North West Leicestershire","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-26","since_iso":"2026-08-27","distress":false,"headline":"LGR Section 24 direction (expected January 2027) will require shadow authority approval for major contracts.","lead":"Dual-approval requirements will slow procurement and project approval from January 2027 onwards. This creates risk to capital programme delivery and service change initiatives during a period of significant organisational restructuring, when speed and\u2026","also":["48 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Corporate Scrutiny Committee on 2026-10-15","n_findings":49,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"General Fund Finance Update - 2026/27 Quarter 1","top_paper_url":"https://nwleics.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s52443/52443.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":156,"news_drafts_total":2}}